    {
      "id": "1878932dbbef20bd2d7b4c70413688613643576b",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1544919567,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> I can revert it for macOS only but it would be interesting to understand why enabling the libc `timegm()` causes the tests to fail on that platform.\n\nIt's because `timegm()` on, at minimum, macOS 13.4 and FreeBSD 13.2 - and probably other versions of those OSes and quite possibly various versions of other OSes - when passed a `struct tm` value for 2013-03-01 00:00:00, returns the correct epoch time for 2013-03-01 00:00:00 UTC, *but* sets `errno` to `EOVERFLOW`.  This causes `tvb_get_string_time()` to think `mktime_utc()` has failed.\n\nI'm changing `mktime_utc()` to save and restore `errno`; I've not found any man pages that mention `timegm()` that make any commitments either to leaving `errno` alone or setting it to any particular value for any particular error, and the code we use on platforms that *don't* have `timegm()` leaves it alone, so I'm adding \"leave ~~Britney~~`errno` alone!\" to `mktime_utc()`'s contract.\n\n(I'm also cleaning up `tvb_get_string_time()` to do a better job of recognizing errors; it might malfunction in some error cases.  It should probably also report an error if `timegm()` returns -1.)",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2023-09-06T07:32:20.851Z",
          "updated_at": "2023-09-06T15:52:22.431Z",
